# isopod runtime tiers — how `isopod doctor` classifies the configured runtime
# (the hardening.conf `runtime` directive / ISOPOD_RUNTIME), whether `isopod
# create` sizes a default --memory for it, and which runtime it auto-selects.
#
#   tier 2  syscall sandbox, shared host kernel, no /dev/kvm needed (e.g. gVisor)
#   tier 3  microVM, separate guest kernel, needs /dev/kvm (e.g. Kata, krun)
#
# "needs /dev/kvm" is a Linux statement. On macOS there is no /dev/kvm — the
# hardware-virt backend is Apple's Hypervisor.framework (sysctl kern.hv_support),
# and boxes already run inside the podman machine / Docker Desktop VM (itself a
# hardware VM). A NESTED Tier 3 runtime there needs an Apple M3+ chip on macOS 15+
# plus nested-virt support, so `isopod doctor` treats Tier-3-in-a-box as N/A on
# macOS and points at the engine VM as the boundary already in force.
#
# Columns: <runtime-name> <tier> <net>. <net> is the guest network stack, which
# decides whether SSH TCP-forwarding and bulk transfers work — VSCodium
# Remote-SSH (`isopod code`) and `isopod copy`/`export` all depend on both:
#   virtio    real virtio-net interface — forwarding and bulk transfers work
#   netstack  gVisor user-space netstack (Tier 2) — works
#   tsi       libkrun Transparent Socket Impersonation (krun's DEFAULT) — SSH
#             exec channels are fast, but bulk data over an SSH TCP port-forward
#             into a guest loopback service STALLS. That is exactly how VSCodium/
#             Cursor/JetBrains reach their remote server, so `isopod code`
#             (Remote-SSH) can't connect over TSI. Upstream libkrun bugs:
#             containers/libkrun#579 (TSI drops large transfers — BufDescTooSmall)
#             + #510 (guest loopback wrongly proxied). isopod avoids TSI by
#             running krun with passt (krun.use_passt=1), a real virtio-net stack
#             — so krun is listed as `virtio` below, not `tsi`. (No runtime is
#             `tsi` today; the classification stays documented for reference.)
#
# The name matches what you pass to --runtime (and the `runtime` directive /
# ISOPOD_RUNTIME). Unlisted runtimes still work; doctor just cannot name their
# tier and create skips the memory default.
runsc         2  netstack
runsc-kvm     2  netstack
kata          3  virtio
kata-runtime  3  virtio
kata-qemu     3  virtio
kata-fc       3  virtio
kata-clh      3  virtio
krun          3  virtio  # via passt (krun.use_passt=1); libkrun default is TSI

# NOT listed: crun-vm. It runs VM DISK images (a "containerdisk", a --rootfs disk
# image, or a bootc bootable container), not a plain OCI container image — which
# is what isopod builds and hands the runtime. It cannot boot an isopod box, so it
# is neither auto-selected nor suggested. kata is the supported Tier 3 microVM.
